    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "It is not uncommon in plugins to want to have an action that:\n- Requires a user input before being performed.\n- Needs to be dot-repeatable: ask once, `.` should repeat without re-asking.\n- Should work with multicursor.\n\nThe general guide is to follow an operator route of \"expression mapping that sets 'operatorfunc' and returns `'g@'`\", but:\n1. Return `'g@l'` to not ask for the textobject (if action is not an actual operator).\n2. Cache user input to not re-ask when dot-repeating.\n\nThis currently poses a problem with multicursor (and `:h edit-repeat`) support due to a documented limitation:\n\n```\n- An 'operatorfunc' that caches its |getchar()| input instead of re-reading it\n  misbehaves at the extra cursors: the unconsumed input runs as a normal-mode\n  key.\n```\n\n---\n\nTo make things more grounded, here is a simplified demo:\n\n```lua\nlocal user_input\nlocal rhs = function()\n  -- Reset cached user input to trigger asking for it.\n  -- The input is usually asked via `vim.fn.getcharstr` or `vim.fn.input()`,\n  -- but there can be other custom synchronous user input method (like\n  -- 'mini.input' or via https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40275).\n  user_input = nil\n\n  vim.o.operatorfunc = function()\n    -- Ensure cached user input is up to date\n    user_input = user_input or vim.fn.input({ prompt = 'Input: ' })\n\n    -- Simple text edit for demo purposes\n    local pos = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n    local text = { user_input .. user_input }\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, pos[1] - 1, pos[2], pos[1] - 1, pos[2], text)\n  end\n\n  return 'g@l'\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'sd', rhs, { expr = true })\n\n-- Add this to `init.lua` for an easier and more informative repro\nvim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { 'aaa', 'bbb' })\n_G.log = {}\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('CmdAtom', { callback = function(ev) table.insert(_G.log, ev.data) end })\n```\n\nThis currently works as: type `sd` to initiate, type+confirm input (like `o<CR>`), it adds the doubled input at cursor. Pressing `.` adds the same doubled input at cursor *because it replays `g@l` portion*. While doing this with active multicursor or `:h edit-repeat` will *replay `g@lo\\r`*. This mismatch is a problem.\n\nOne solution that (currently) works here is to resolve user input *during expression mapping evaluation* (i.e. before returning `g@l`). **However**, it hits `:h map-expression` limitations (textlock in current and other buffers, etc.). It currently seems to work fine for a pure `vim.fn.getcharstr`, but not for something more complicated: `vim.fn.input` with ui2 enabled (#41654) or custom implementations that edit text in other buffers to visualize input.\n\n---\n\nWhat can be done here? There are several approaches that I can think of (from most to least favorable in my opinion):\n\n1. Prefer using unresolved `ev.data.lhs` when replaying in multicursor and `:h edit-repeat` instead of resolved `ev.data.keys`. In this example it will lead to replaying `sdo\\r` which should work correctly (`sd` will reset `user_input` and ask+consume `o<CR>`).\n\n2. Lift the `:h map-expression` limitatinons and make plugins resolve user input during expression mapping. This is (probably) what's required for #41654. Ideally also needs a test in `mcursor_spec.lua` that resolving user input in expression mapping will work with multicursor.\n\n3. Make dot-repeat replay with the user input. This seems to be very breaking.\n\n(@justinmk, I tried to make this as concise as I could)\n",
