            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Unfortunately, there is also a case of a true operator (action that asks for textobject) that can not benefit from resolving user input during expression mapping.\n\nThe operator that acts on textobject/motion and requires a user input usually asks for exactly that order of keys. The most prominent case here is \"add surrounding\" operator: `<surround-map>` + `<textobject>` + `<surround-id>`. Like `saiw)` in 'mini.surround' or `ysiw)` in `vim-surround`.\n\nThe necessity of this order means that user input for surrounding id *must* be done after resolving expression mapping (after resolving textobject, in fact).\n\nHere is an example:\n\n```lua\nlocal user_input\nlocal rhs = function()\n  user_input = nil\n\n  vim.o.operatorfunc = function()\n    user_input = user_input or vim.fn.input({ prompt = 'Input: ' })\n\n    -- Simple \"add same surrounding\"\n    local right = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, ']')\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, right[1] - 1, right[2] + 1, right[1] - 1, right[2] + 1, { user_input })\n\n    local left = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, '[')\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, left[1] - 1, left[2], left[1] - 1, left[2], { user_input })\n  end\n\n  return 'g@'\nend\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'sa', rhs, { expr = true })\n```\n\nHere typing `sa` - `iw` - `o<CR>` wraps inner word with letters `o`. This is dot-repeatable, but doesn't work in multicursor since `CmdAtom` has resolved `keys` as `\"g@iwo\\r\"`.\n\nResolving user input would change the order to `sa` - `o<CR>` - `iw`. Which works, but doesn't fit the established convention.\n\n---\n\nAs resolving the keys during expression mapping is not the full proof approach, this limits my current ideas only to preferring `CmdAtom.data.lhs` over `CmdAtom.data.keys`.\n\nAdjusting the operator to conditionally cache user input (like during multicursor cascade, which indeed is a bit different from checking for present cursors when doing dot-repeat) would *probably* work for multicursor, but not *quite* for `:h edit-repeat`. The latter would require temporarily setting custom flag (like `_G.is_super_dot_repeat = true`) and making sure that operator respects it (which hard to do if operator is not owned by the user).",
